## 4. Shiki Dual-Theming & Code Transformation

Below is an example of code blocks with built-in Shiki transformer support for notation diffs (`[!code ++]`, `[!code --]`) and line highlights (`[!code highlight]`):

```typescript
// Compiler configuration demonstration
export interface PipelineOptions {
  inlineAssets: boolean;
  dualTheme: 'github-light' | 'github-dark'; // [!code highlight]
  targetProtocol: 'file://' | 'https://';
}

export function createOfflineBundle(opts: PipelineOptions) {
  const legacyLoader = false; // [!code --]
  const singleFileBundle = true; // [!code ++]
  return {
    ready: singleFileBundle,
    target: opts.targetProtocol
  };
}
```

### Interactive Editable Placeholders

Any `<placeholder_name>` pattern inside code blocks is automatically detected and converted into an editable inline input. Readers can supply their own parameters (such as API keys, hostnames, or tenant names) and click the **Copy** button to copy the snippet with their customized values filled in.

```bash
# Set your deployment variables
export CLUSTER_NAME=<cluster_name>
export DEPLOYMENT_ENV=<environment>
export API_ENDPOINT=https://<api_host>/v1

# Authenticate agent
curl -X POST "$API_ENDPOINT/auth" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer <auth_token>" \
  -d '{"cluster": "'"$CLUSTER_NAME"'"}'
```

